WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.648
Parliament can amend the

2
00:00:01.648 --> 00:00:02.405
Constitution,

3
00:00:02.505 --> 00:00:04.083
even fundamental rights.

4
00:00:04.383 --> 00:00:06.047
So can it rewrite the whole thing?

5
00:00:06.547 --> 00:00:07.294
No.

6
00:00:07.594 --> 00:00:09.279
Not its basic structure.

7
00:00:09.729 --> 00:00:13.235
That line was drawn in Kesavananda

8
00:00:13.235 --> 00:00:15.916
Bharati, on 24 April 1973,

9
00:00:16.036 --> 00:00:17.792
by thirteen judges, the largest

10
00:00:17.792 --> 00:00:19.151
bench the Court has sat.

11
00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:21.024
By seven to six, it held

12
00:00:21.024 --> 00:00:23.099
that Parliament may amend any part,

13
00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:25.263
but cannot alter the Constitution's

14
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:26.207
basic structure.

15
00:00:26.657 --> 00:00:28.457
It overruled Golaknath, which

16
00:00:28.457 --> 00:00:30.133
had said fundamental rights

17
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:31.436
could not be amended.

18
00:00:31.886 --> 00:00:33.577
In Minerva Mills, in 1980,

19
00:00:33.577 --> 00:00:34.617
the Court added:

20
00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:36.118
a limited amending power

21
00:00:36.118 --> 00:00:38.045
is itself part of that structure.

22
00:00:38.495 --> 00:00:39.881
Amend the text, yes.

23
00:00:40.081 --> 00:00:41.831
Change its identity, no.

24
00:00:42.331 --> 00:00:43.205
Your turn.

25
00:00:43.355 --> 00:00:44.669
Which case first set out

26
00:00:44.669 --> 00:00:46.257
the basic structure doctrine?
